# Optional speech backend: words a dictionary does not have, and which reading of a heteronym a sentence means.
#
# To SET UP, not to regenerate: `py -3.13 tools/bootstrap.py --only speech-venv` creates python-speech/, installs the
# lock below into it with --require-hashes and --no-deps, fetches NLTK's data pinned into models/nltk_data/, and
# smoke-tests src/speech/g2p.py. The commands that follow are for regenerating the lock.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE ASK. `requirements.lock.txt` beside it is the ANSWER, generated by a real resolution and
# hash-pinned; never hand-edit it. It was made with uv (the webapp venv carries it), resolving for 3.13 on every
# platform at once:
#
#     python/Scripts/uv.exe pip compile src/speech/requirements.in --universal --python-version 3.13 \
#         --generate-hashes --no-emit-package distance --extra-index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu \
#         --index-strategy unsafe-best-match --emit-index-url --output-file src/speech/requirements.lock.txt
#
# `--emit-index-url` keeps PyTorch's index in the lock, which bootstrap installs from alone; `unsafe-best-match` lets
# the resolver take `+cpu` builds from it while the rest comes from PyPI. With --require-hashes, a second index
# cannot substitute anything: every file must match a listed hash.
#
# **`--no-emit-package distance`, and therefore `--no-deps` at install.** g2p-en declares `distance`, which PyPI has
# only as a source archive, against bootstrap's wheels-only rule; g2p-en never imports it (its imports are nltk,
# numpy and its own `expand`). Leaving it out of the lock means pip must not resolve dependencies itself at install,
# or it would go looking for it; the lock is complete, so `--no-deps` installs exactly what is listed.
#
# Install into `python-speech/` at the repository root, not `python-mediapipe/` (vision) or the others: machine-
# learning stacks pin conflicting versions, which is why every backend has its own venv (see src/vision).
#
# NOT required to build or run the studio. Motion.speech works without it from the CMU Pronouncing Dictionary alone;
# this is for the words that dictionary lacks and the sentences it cannot read.

# The grapheme-to-phoneme model (Kyubyong Park, Apache 2.0): CMUdict for known words, a small GRU in plain NumPy for
# the rest, and a homograph table chosen between by NLTK's part-of-speech tag. 2.1.0 (2019) is its last release.
g2p-en==2.1.0

# 3.9 and later load the tagger from JSON, not a pickle (the change came with CVE-2024-39705's fix); refuse older.
nltk>=3.9

# g2p-en's own dependencies, resolved rather than pinned here: numpy for the model, inflect for spelling numbers out.
numpy
inflect

# Forced alignment (src/speech/align.py): when each word of a known line is said in a recording. wav2vec 2.0's English
# letters model (torchaudio's WAV2VEC2_ASR_BASE_960H bundle; weights pinned in tools/bootstrap.py) and torchaudio's CTC
# forced_align, which torchaudio 2.10 kept when it entered maintenance (pytorch/audio#3902). Its audio I/O moved to
# TorchCodec and FFmpeg; align.py does not use it, reading the WAV with Python's own `wave` module.
#
# CPU builds from PyTorch's own index, as src/vision does and for the same reasons: PyPI's torch is a CUDA build on
# Linux, gigabytes the container never uses. Same torch as the vision lock, so the two venvs agree.
torch==2.14.0+cpu
torchaudio
